te powdery colonies on  any of these tissues and should you  suspect powdery mildew in any case you  can remove the question will shoot place  it inside of a ziplock bag with a  slightly damp paper towel to kind of  keep the humidity up stored at room  temperature in a spot that gets a daily  light dark cycle and then check for  production of spores one one one day and  two days later and that would be a wait  there's a powdery mildew present there  you're gonna start to see production of  spores you'll see sporulation so some of  the cultural practices that you can do  this these cultural practices are the  legwork to make your fungicides work  better they're to take pressure off of  your fungicides being kind of the last  line of defense and kind of take  stress off your fungicides and so these  are these are things that you should  always be doing each season one of them  pruning so this is the act of cutting  back the first wave of growth and you  shouldn't do this here first or probably  your second year of production but once  your varieties start to get a little bit  more vigorous in their growth you cut  back that first wave of shoots that  emerge from the ground and this cuts  away any potentially infected hot  material that is emerging and parading  powdery mildew or downy mildew or any  other possible diseases the frequency  and the intensity of your pruning as  dependent upon the age of your yard and  also the vigor of your varieties how  bigger ously each of your varieties is  growing generally mechanical pruning is  more effective but there's also just the  fact that any pruning is better than no  pruning in terms of training times it is  best to try and get your shoots out of  that basil foliage as soon as possible I  know that there are their timing  considerations in terms of reaching the  top of the trellis to produce production  of lateral branches but the sooner you  can get your primary kind of most  optimal shoots up and trained away from  all the basal foliage the sooner they'll  be away from kind of that micro climate  where powdery mildew and downy mildew  are likely to kind of rear their heads  the last kind of cultural practice to  consider is stripping the lower leaves  so this is breaking the green bridge and  so this picture here you can see this  green bridge of green hops shoots and  leaves that if there was any sort of  disease going on along the ground here  this would be an easy Highway for the  pathogen to spread up these two adjacent  plants and so again you probably  wouldn't want to do this the first  couple years of your yard but once your  plants are growing vigorously and once  they reach a certain height so about ten  feet you could strip off the lower three  feet of growth and this breaks that  green bridge and also  induces some airflow along the soil line  which removes that micro high humidity  microclimate associated with kind of a  basal foliage so the last thing to  comment on is fungicides in terms of  management and so it's important to know  your fungicides know which fungicides  apply to downy mildew versus powdery  mildew and check the label if you're  unsure there are many applications  students to make you on the label and  you want to save your big guns for your  most critical management periods  especially that time around bloom so  these are fungicides that are generally  specific to powdery mildew I won't go  into them into detail but they're here  as examples and as a resource free to  use again these are examples the product  should always be used according to the  label in a given state and you should  make contact with your Cooperative  Extension personnel or your regional  personnel and make sure that each  product is labeled for hops so these  next few slides are credit to Dave Jen  out of Oregon State and this is another  just resource that I wanted to include  in the PowerPoint deck as more examples  of fungicides that are available for use  but again check whether they're  available and legal to use in your area  so this slide here again provided by  Dave is intended to show that really  under high disease pressure high  pressure of powdery mildew is when you  start to see a discrepancy in how your  fungicides perform but if you can keep  your disease pressure your powdery  mildew pressure moderate to low which is  done through kind of being very good  about your cultural practices all of  your fungicides perform quite well in  comparison till the untreated and so  this is really what you're aiming for is  to be good about your cultural practices  and keep it under this moderate pressure  so that you don't need to be relying  quite as heavily on fungicides to be the  factor that is keeping your percent cone  infection at a satisfactory level  this slide here is also from Dave and it  is here as a resource and is intended to  provide some context in terms of timing  of fungicide applications the important  things to note here are that some of our  bigger guns such as the ovando or Quinn  Tech or Luna and pristine they're all  around flowering and they're they're  used at that time to protect the hop  cones as much as possible because that  is the direct harvestable product that  we're trying to protect  and so you time them around at that time  and so with that I want to thank you all  for your time I have my contact  information listed here feel free to  email me with any follow-up questions  that you may have and I will be happy to  get in touch with you thank you very  much
 

